From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] accel/rocket: wait for a running IRQ handler before
 resetting a core

rocket_reset() calls drm_sched_stop(), which stops the scheduler and
returns.  It does not wait for a threaded handler that is already
running, so the comment that followed it -- "Remaining interrupts have
been handled" -- stated an assumption rather than something the code
arranged.  A completion handler that is mid-flight when the reset starts
keeps running against a core the reset is about to put through
rocket_core_reset(), and against an in_flight_job the reset has already
dropped.

Call synchronize_irq(core->irq) after drm_sched_stop() and reword the
comment to say what holds afterwards.

Mask the block first.  rocket_job_hw_submit() arms INTERRUPT_MASK on
every submit -- the DMA-error pair on RK3576, the DPU pair on RK3588 --
and only the hardirq clears it, so on an ordinary timeout the mask is
still live and an interrupt can arrive after the sync returns.  Waiting
without masking fences the handler that is running, not the one the
hardware is still entitled to raise.  The next submit re-arms the mask,
so nothing is lost by clearing it here.

Clear what is latched alongside it, the same set the completion path
clears -- 0x1ffff, and PC_DONE_0/1 as well where the completion is
polled.  Masking ends nothing already raised.  On RK3588 that matters
because the hardirq decides on INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS alone and the line
is IRQF_SHARED, so another device's interrupt finds the stale bit and
wakes the threaded handler again.  Here it matters for the poll instead:
0025 reads those same raw bits to decide the writing block has finished,
so a bit left standing across a reset is a completion the next job's
first tick can read as its own.  0024's sequence number does not cover
that one -- it fences a stale work item, not a stale register.

That write is guarded by pm_runtime_get_if_active(), and it needs to be.
It is the first register access rocket_reset() makes, and the function
holds no runtime PM reference of its own: the only one in the window
belongs to in_flight_job, and the completion path can have put it and
cleared the pointer before the timeout worker arrives.  drm_sched_stop()
sits in between, can block, and subtracts every pending job's credits,
so rocket_job_is_idle() is true and nothing keeps the core resumed.  A
register access with the domain down takes an async SError on this
hardware, so the reset must not be what causes one.  pm_runtime_active()
would not do: it is a snapshot that holds no reference, and the domain
can drop between the test and the write.

Nor is > 0 shorthand for != 0.  pm_runtime_get_if_active() tests
power.disable_depth before power.runtime_status, so -EINVAL masks a
suspended device rather than excluding one, and this driver makes that
state twice: pm_runtime_force_suspend(), its own system sleep callback,
disables runtime PM before it turns the clocks off, and
rocket_core_fini() suspends the core and disables before it cancels the
timeout worker.  Either would put these writes on a powered-down block.

It has to go before the scoped_guard(mutex, &core->job_lock) rather than
inside it.  rocket_job_handle_irq() takes job_lock -- 0027 moved the
register writes under it, so it now holds that lock for the whole
handler -- and waiting for the handler while holding the lock it wants
would deadlock instead of fencing anything.  Nothing is held at that
point, and both callers, rocket_job_timedout() and rocket_reset_work(),
run in process context, so sleeping there is allowed.

The other way into rocket_job_handle_irq() on this SoC is the completion
poll work, which the hrtimer_cancel()/cancel_work_sync() pair above
already fences; this closes the shared interrupt thread, which the
RK3576 still takes for the DMA-error bits.

This does not stop a handler that has already read in_flight_job from
finishing its work on the job the reset is about to drop.  That window
needs the check and the register writes to be one step under the lock,
which is what 0027 does; the two are complementary.

From this series -- Jiaxing Hu's patch, suggested by Igor Paunovic,
tracked at its v12 posting, where the raw-status clear was added.  The
function is shared with the RK3588, which carries the same fix as
rocket/091.

  https://lore.kernel.org/linux-rockchip/20260817113603.1436067-3-gahing@gahingwoo.com/

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c | 59 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 56 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
index 4ce6564c9..7a5a60c1f 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -787,9 +787,62 @@ rocket_reset(struct rocket_core *core, struct drm_sched_job *bad)
 	drm_sched_stop(&core->sched, bad);
 
 	/*
-	 * Remaining interrupts have been handled, but we might still have
-	 * stuck jobs. Let's make sure the PM counters stay balanced by
-	 * manually calling pm_runtime_put_noidle().
+	 * Mask the block before waiting.  rocket_job_hw_submit() arms
+	 * INTERRUPT_MASK on every submit -- the DMA-error pair on RK3576, the
+	 * DPU pair on RK3588 -- and only the hardirq clears it, so on an
+	 * ordinary timeout it is still live and an interrupt can arrive after
+	 * the sync below returns.  The next submit re-arms it, so nothing is
+	 * lost here.
+	 *
+	 * Only when the device is already awake, though.  This function holds
+	 * no runtime PM reference of its own: the only one in the window
+	 * belongs to in_flight_job, and the completion path may have put it
+	 * and cleared the pointer before the timeout worker got here.
+	 * drm_sched_stop() above can block for a long time, and it drops
+	 * every pending job's credits, so rocket_job_is_idle() is true and
+	 * nothing keeps the core resumed.  On this hardware a register access
+	 * with the domain down takes an async SError, so a reset must not be
+	 * the thing that causes one.
+	 *
+	 * Only a positive answer will do.  pm_runtime_get_if_active() tests
+	 * power.disable_depth before power.runtime_status, so -EINVAL MASKS a
+	 * suspended device rather than excluding one, and this driver makes
+	 * that state in both pm_runtime_force_suspend() and
+	 * rocket_core_fini().
+	 *
+	 * Clear what is latched along with the mask, the set the completion
+	 * path clears.  Masking ends nothing already raised, and on this SoC
+	 * the completion poll reads those bits out of INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS
+	 * directly, so a bit left standing across a reset is a completion the
+	 * next job's first poll tick can read as its own.
+	 */
+	if (pm_runtime_get_if_active(core->dev) > 0) {
+		u32 clear = 0x1ffff;
+
+		if (core->soc->poll_completion)
+			clear |= PC_INTERRUPT_MASK_RK3576_PC_DONE_0 |
+				 PC_INTERRUPT_MASK_RK3576_PC_DONE_1;
+
+		rocket_pc_writel(core, INTERRUPT_MASK, 0x0);
+		rocket_pc_writel(core, INTERRUPT_CLEAR, clear);
+		pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(core->dev);
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * drm_sched_stop() returns without waiting for a threaded handler that
+	 * is already running, so wait for one here.  This has to stay outside
+	 * job_lock: rocket_job_handle_irq() takes that lock, so waiting for the
+	 * handler while holding it would be waiting for a handler that is
+	 * waiting for us.  Nothing is held at this point, and both callers run
+	 * in process context, so sleeping here is allowed.  The polled
+	 * completion is already fenced above by cancel_work_sync().
+	 */
+	synchronize_irq(core->irq);
+
+	/*
+	 * No handler is running now, but we might still have stuck jobs. Let's
+	 * make sure the PM counters stay balanced by manually calling
+	 * pm_runtime_put_noidle().
 	 */
 	scoped_guard(mutex, &core->job_lock) {
 		if (core->in_flight_job)
-- 
